   "date": "1939-02-14",
   "from": "Joseph C. Grew, American Embassy, Tokyo",
   "to": "U.S. Department of State",
   "about": "Grew assesses whether trade restrictions and gold controls could undermine Japan’s economy.",
   "summary": "The surviving pages tabulate gold shipments to the United States and examine Japan’s gold reserves, import requirements, strategic stocks and foreign exchange. Grew challenges parts of a British Embassy memorandum and an analysis from the Embassy at Chungking, while acknowledging that a broad embargo on Japanese exports could be disastrous. He concludes that the statistics do not prove sanctions would collapse Japan’s economy and warns that Japan could respond with a wartime state of mind.",
